<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arabian Sea</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Arabian Sea is the part of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>. It carried maritime trade linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> and Indian ports. It was important to the trade of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a>, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire</a>'s coastal ambitions and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a>, whose navy operated along <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s western coast.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arabian-sea.jpg" alt="Arabian Sea" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aplaice · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arabian_Sea_in_its_region.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Persian Gulf trade through Bushehr and Bandar Abbas continued, and Nader's intervention in Oman, culminating in the capture of Muscat around 1743, extended Iranian reach into the <b>Arabian Sea</b>, but these revenues were insufficient to sustain an empire held together by military force.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Tughluq armies failed to recover the region, and by his death in 1358 Bahman Shah controlled a kingdom stretching from the <b>Arabian Sea</b> coast to the borders of Telangana, bounded in the south by the Krishna and Tungabhadra rivers, where it faced the newly founded Vijayanagara Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Chandragupta II (c. 375–415 CE) extended the empire westward by defeating the Shaka satraps of western India, giving the Guptas access to the prosperous port cities of Gujarat and the <b>Arabian Sea</b> trade routes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Internal trade flowed along Deccan routes, while Konkan ports such as Dabhol linked the Marathas to the <b>Arabian Sea</b> trade in cotton, spices and the horses essential for cavalry.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>By uniting the western and eastern Deccan, they linked the <b>Arabian Sea</b> ports, which traded with the Roman world, to the Krishna delta and the Bay of Bengal, from which trade and Buddhist teachers reached Sri Lanka and Southeast Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/satavahana-dynasty">Satavahana dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Pan-Arabism, in Arabic al-waḥda al-ʿarabiyya, “Arab unity”, espouses the unification of all Arab people in a single nation-state made up of the Arab countries of West Asia and North Africa, from the Atlantic Ocean to the <b>Arabian Sea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/pan-arabism">Pan-Arabism · Core Ideas</a></li></ul></section>
